  "review": {
    "distinctions": "Dense runtime arithmetic, finite-word projection, symbolic prime specification, independent arithmetic replay and source search have separate scopes. The bit counts are actual binary magnitude lengths; packed byte counts exclude the allocator and scratch space. The 17-times-wider integer is composite. The dense runs do not find a new world record prime.",
    "full_width": "The release supplies the actual prime at k=972. The wider k=1..972 table is our reconstructed audit family. All positive low-word verdicts have complete order witnesses. For all 384 narrower widths at least one decision is wrong in that family.",
    "negative_control": "The standard compiler rejected using a proof of Nat.Prime 17 for Nat.Prime (2^64+17) with a type mismatch. A first automatic trial for the separate residue-compositeness proof reached maxRecDepth and produced an error; the final file supplies a divisor explicitly.",
    "release_scope": "The code scan covers every .lean file under src at the stated release commit. It covers only explicit spellings in its recorded regex. The 385-bit Lucas example was separately tested before and after narrowing. Neither check is a full audit of the proof or runtime.",
